Subject: filling holes in its series
Message-ID: <008e01c3b936$5f2a8320$daeefea9@irma>

Hi,

I have two irregular time series, say its1 and 
its2. The second is a "time-subset" of the first in the sense that dates in 
its2 are all present in the first but the converse is not true. I would like to 
fill the "holes" in its2 so the two series are the same lenght. Moreover, when 
filling the hole, the new value in its2 should be the same as the 
"preceding" in its2 also. Example:

 

its1

1/1/1970    10

1/2/1970    20

1/4/1970    30

1/7/1970    40

 

its2

1/1/1970    1

1/4/1970    3

 

the result should be:

 

its2

1/1/1970    1

1/2/1970    1

1/4/1970    3

1/7/1970    3

 

Is there an elegant and efficient way to do that in 
S-Plus? (I'm using S-Plus 4.5 under Windows 2000).

Thank you very much,

Patrick

From bert_gunter at merck.com  Wed Dec  3 14:09:37 2003
From: bert_gunter at merck.com (Gunter, Bert)
Date: Wed, 03 Dec 2003 09:09:37 -0500
Subject: FW: filling holes in its series
Message-ID: <D45BC82E21E16149AC5A3F706350626405AB0B8D@usrymx14.merck.com>

 One approach is to 
use match() and diff(). I'm not exactly sure how time series work in 
S-Plus 4.5 (I use R now), but you should be able to do something like 
this:

 

Assume its1 and its2 are vectors in date increasing order, and that 
dates1 and dates2 are the corresponding dates as character strings. 
Then:

 

# create a "times" vector for rep 

z<- match(dates2,dates1)  ## dates must be in increasing 
order!

times<- c(diff(z), 
length(dates1)-z[length(dates2)]+1)

filledvec<- rep(its2,times)

 

Cheers,

