From bert_gunter at merck.com  Tue Dec  9 13:49:25 2003
From: bert_gunter at merck.com (Gunter, Bert)
Date: Tue, 09 Dec 2003 08:49:25 -0500
Subject: Re: Help on comparing models
Message-ID: <D45BC82E21E16149AC5A3F706350626405AB0BB2@usrymx14.merck.com>

No. 
anova() only works for testing nested models. 

 

So 
this turns out to be another one of those innocent, "simple" questons beneath 
which lies a den of statistical snakes. There is a large literature on this 
issue. Akaike/Bayes Information Criteria (AIC,BIC) obtain measures of fit that 
attempt to account for the number of parameters by a penalization criteria; more 
recently, so-called Bayesian model choice methodology has received a lot of 
attention, and might be relevant; "LAR" (Least Angle Regression) is another new 
technique for (implicit) penalization that might be useful.

 

Note 
the structure of the problem: You have 7 variables to choose from: a constant, 
the 5 terms of the full quadratic in long and lat in fit1, and the exponential 
in fit2. You wish to fit a parsimonious linear model in these variables. 
Some form of cross validation would probably be useful to help you do this and 
avoid overfitting, though the novel (to my knowledge) aspect of your question is 
that you want to restrict the search to a subspace of models rather than 
allowing any possible subset of the variables.

 

Perhaps those more expert in this area may be stimulated 
by my inaccuracies and naivites to elaborate more fully on these issues.I 
think they ultimately impinge on the philosophy of science: There is 
no fully "objective" data-driven (statistical) criterion to resolve 
such questions. The "right" model is ultimately what the high priests of the 
subject matter area (e.g.the "scientific experts") agree on. Obviously, all 
should feel free to vehemently disagree with this view (but see 
Feyerabend's work for a much more principled and eloquent statement of 
it).

 

I need 
to emphasize: My opinions only -- this has nothing to do with my company or poor 
colleagues, who should not be tarred by my statistical 
brush.

 

Cheers,

