To 
add a bit to Bert's comments:

 

IMHO 
Statistics is rarely about "proving some hypothesis".  The statistical 
tests of hypotheses gives probability statements about a very precise 
mathematical construct, of which "fit1 is better than fit2" is as far from as I 
could imagine, and one does not get "proof" from the test, just probability 
statements.  You need to state precisely what you mean by "better" before 
an attempt is even possible.

 

Leo 
Breiman in his controversial "Two Culture" paper in Statistical Science 
expressed his view that more accurate models (in terms of prediction on future 
data) have a better chance of approximating the truth than less accurate 
ones.  If you buy that, you can use either CV (as Bert suggested) or 
bootstrap to help you decide between the two models.

 

HTH,

Andy

